#doc (Manual) "Content Security Policy" =>

Every HTML page generated by Verso now includes a content security policy as a `<meta>` tag that restricts which scripts the page runs.
This is a breaking change for sites that add scripts outside the standard mechanisms of their genre.

The default policy allows scripts from the site's own origin, browser-processed inline scripts by their SHA-256 hashes, and the origins of external scripts in structured HTML.
It doesn't restrict styles, images, fonts, connections, frames, or embedded objects.

:::paragraph
Browsers block the following items. Each blocked script is reported in the console:

* Scripts inside raw HTML strings
* Inline event handler attributes such as `onclick`
* `javascript:` URLs
* Scripts that load further scripts from origins that are not listed in the policy.
:::

Scripts that are added through the standard mechanisms, such as {name}`HtmlAssets.extraJs`, {name}`HtmlAssets.extraJsFiles`, and `<script>` elements in {name}`HtmlConfig.extraHead` in the `Manual` genre, are automatically allowed by the policy.
Custom templates need a leading structured HTML head when the policy is enabled. Trusted inline script bodies can be authorized explicitly in the policy configuration.
